Optimal Solution - Use dynamic programming to count decoding ways by validating one-digit and two-digit mappings at each step.
                  At each index, I check whether the last one or two characters form valid encodings and build the solution bottom-up.

class Solution {
    public int numDecodings(String s) {
        int n = s.length();
        // dp[i] = number of ways to decode substring s[0..i-1]
        int[] dp = new int[n + 1];
        // Base case: empty string has 1 way to decode
        dp[0] = 1;
        // First character cannot be '0'
        dp[1] = (s.charAt(0) == '0') ? 0 : 1;
        // Fill DP table
        for (int i = 2; i <= n; i++) {
            // Decode last one digit
            int oneDigit = Integer.parseInt(s.substring(i - 1, i));
            // Decode last two digits
            int twoDigit = Integer.parseInt(s.substring(i - 2, i));
            // If single digit is valid (1–9), add ways from dp[i-1]
            if (oneDigit >= 1) {
                dp[i] += dp[i - 1];
            }
            // If two-digit number is valid (10–26), add ways from dp[i-2]
            if (twoDigit >= 10 && twoDigit <= 26) {
                dp[i] += dp[i - 2];
            }
        }
        // Total ways to decode the full string
        return dp[n];
    }
}


Time Complexity :- O(N).
Space Complexity :- O(N).
